# Flask Rules

## Core Principles
- Flask is a microframework based on Werkzeug and Jinja2
- Follow the separation of concerns (MVC pattern)
- Use Flask's application factory pattern for larger applications
- Keep routes and view functions organized
- Use blueprints to organize your application

## Application Structure
- Use application factory pattern (`create_app()` function)
- Organize code into logical modules (blueprints)
- Keep configuration separate from application code
- Use environment variables for sensitive configuration
- Place templates and static files in their respective directories

## Routing
- Keep URL routes clean and intuitive
- Use appropriate HTTP methods for different operations
- Include trailing slashes consistently
- Use URL converters for dynamic parts of routes
- Leverage `url_for()` instead of hardcoding URLs

## View Functions
- Keep view functions focused on a single responsibility
- Validate incoming data
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes
- Use decorators for cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging)
- Handle errors explicitly

## Templates
- Use Jinja2 templates for HTML generation
- Escape user-provided content to prevent XSS attacks
- Create a base template and extend it
- Use template inheritance for consistent layouts
- Keep business logic out of templates

## Forms
- Use Flask-WTF for form handling and validation
- Define form classes separately from routes
- Include CSRF protection
- Validate form data before processing
- Display validation errors to users

## Database
- Use Flask-SQLAlchemy for database operations
- Create database models for your data
- Use migrations (Flask-Migrate) to manage schema changes
- Keep database operations outside of view functions
- Implement proper error handling for database operations

## Authentication
- Store passwords securely (use password hashing)
- Implement session-based or token-based authentication
- Protect sensitive routes with login_required decorators
- Use Flask-Login for session management
- Set appropriate cookie security flags

## Security
- Always escape user input to prevent XSS attacks
- Use CSRF protection for forms
- Validate file uploads and use secure_filename
- Set proper Content Security Policy headers
- Keep dependencies updated to avoid vulnerabilities

## Error Handling
- Define custom error handlers for common HTTP errors
- Log errors appropriately
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Return appropriate status codes
- Consider different formats for API errors (JSON vs HTML)

## API Design
- Use RESTful principles for API endpoints
- Return consistent JSON structures
- Include proper HTTP status codes
- Use API versioning
- Implement pagination for large collections

## Testing
- Write tests for your application
- Use Flask's test client for integration tests
- Mock external services in tests
- Test both success and error paths
- Test database operations with a test database

## Performance
- Use caching when appropriate
- Optimize database queries
- Use background tasks for long-running operations
- Consider using async capabilities for IO-bound operations
- Implement proper connection pooling

## Deployment
- Use WSGI servers in production (Gunicorn, uWSGI)
- Set DEBUG=False in production
- Use a reverse proxy (Nginx, Apache)
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Implement logging for production troubleshooting

## Integration with Frontend (React)
- Implement proper CORS handling for API endpoints
- Structure API responses to be easily consumed by React
- Consider using Flask-RESTful or Flask-RESTX for API development
- Use JSON Web Tokens (JWT) for authentication if building a SPA
- Provide proper API documentation

## File Uploads
- Validate file uploads (type, size, content)
- Use secure_filename to sanitize filenames
- Store files outside of the web root if possible
- Consider using cloud storage for production
- Implement proper error handling for upload failures

## Backend Service for Frontend
- The backend API runs at: http://localhost:5000
- Serves both the API endpoints and the static frontend files
- Uses Socket.IO for real-time communication with the frontend
- Integrates with the existing agent code to process queries
- Provides sample data endpoints for testing without the full agent

